> > Short answer: it doesn’t do anything.
> > It’s up to the user to define a model that has the kind of
> > interpretability properties that are desired. Unfortunately, it’s
> > an open question whether that’s always possible.
> >
> > One approach is to impose an orthogonality constraint between the
> > covariate(s) and the random field component on the domain the lgcp
> > is defined on. This can be done via the linear integral constraint
> > options in inla.spde2.matern.
> >
> > Another option is to do a post processing constraint where the
> > covariate-like aspects of the spde are subtracted and moved into
> > the covariate effect.
> >
> > A third option is to specify covariance parameters for the spde
> > that encourages it to avoid spatial structure of the size present
> > in the covariate.
> >
> > A fourth option is to accept the harsh truth that only the combined
> > effect of the covariate and field are identifiable. (This is an
> > important option!)
> >
> > Which of these, or other, options to use depend on the precise
> > problem and scientific questions that are of interest.
