...and, the pardiso.lic file, for which name is fixed, must be there at
the same directory for which you run the inla program from.

within the INLA package, this is taken care of automatically.

when you compile your work, you can check this doing (below, inla.work <http://inla.work>
is just a another build), running the inla-program in a different mode,
like

$ inla.work <http://inla.work> -m pardiso

***********************************************************************
****
CONTAINS Runtime Modules of Parallel Sparse Linear Solver PARDISO Vers.
6.0
CUSTOMIZED FOR THE R-INLA PACKAGE WHICH SOLVES A LARGE CLASS OF
STATISTICAL
MODELS USING THE INLA
APPROACH.
Copyright Universita della Svizzera Italiana 2000-2018 All Rights
Reserved.

No PARDISO license file found.  Please see `
http://www.pardiso-project.org/r-inla
where to place the license file pardiso.lic
***********************************************************************
****
FAILURE: PARDISO IS NOT INSTALLED OR NOT WORKING

$ cp ~/sys/licenses/pardiso.lic .
$ inla.work <http://inla.work> -m pardiso
SUCCESS: PARDISO IS INSTALLED AND WORKING

On Tue, 2019-03-26 at 01:53 +0900, Helpdesk wrote:
> On Mon, 2019-03-25 at 09:33 -0700, Nafis Sadat wrote:
> > I'm guessing that, like you suggested, I have to REMOVE the
> > libpardiso.so file before linking to inla.o (which I might have to
> > hack in through the Makefile). I think I'm still missing a key part
> > in this whole process.
>
> Hi,
>
> the PARDISO integration is kind of experimental for the moment, and
> by
> default, INLA is set up to compile and link with open-source software
> only
>
> to link with the PARDISO library, do
>
> in inlaprog/Makefile, remove in the 'OBJ=' definition, the entry
> 'src/libpardiso.o'
>
> add linking to either
>
> R/x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu-
> library/default/INLA/bin/pardiso/libnopardiso/linux/libpardiso.so
>
> R/x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu-
> library/default/INLA/bin/pardiso/libpardiso/linux/libpardiso.so
>
> which you'll find in your own R-INLA installation.
>
> INLA needs a special version of the PARDISO library, and that is only
> distributed with the INLA package. downloading from their website
> will
> not work.
>
> the PARDISO library in the INLA pacakge, is compiled with gcc-7.2
> compilers (Ubuntu1804) if you want the ones compiled with gcc-4.8
> (Ubuntu1604, centOS7), you have to download the linux binary from
>
> http://inla.r-inla-download.org/Linux-builds/
>
> and use the libpardiso.so  in there.
>
>
> Best
> H
>

--
Håvard Rue
Helpdesk
he...@r-inla.org

