Sounds good. I'm rebuilding my Docker and will try all that you said:

1) First, install R-INLA as is from the pre-built binary.

2) Take the libpardiso.so out of there.

ALTERNATIVELY: get the tarballs from http://inla.r-inla-download.org/Linux-builds/ <http://www.google.com/url?q=http%3A%2F%2Finla.r-inla-download.org%2FLinux-builds%2F&sa=D&sntz=1&usg=AFQjCNGDIBd-CjnNtCDBlO9QLQTXBzNh_w>  and take out the libpardiso.so from there.

3) Clone the R-INLA repo, and update the Makefile to remove 'src/libpardiso.o' from OBJ.

4) Compile R-INLA with my custom Makefile and that will link to the newly acquired `libpardiso.so` from step 2.

5) Then, if all goes well, doing `/path/to/R/library/INLA/bin/inla.run -m pardiso` will give the info on the PARDISO.

Doing a random download of the stable library and going `inla.run -m pardiso` actually gave me a segfault, and so I will dig into this deeper and report back!

Thanks again!

Nafis

